.TH "RTCERROREVENT.RTCERROREVENT" "3JS" "May 13, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
RTCErrorEvent.RTCErrorEvent \- RTCErrorEvent: RTCErrorEvent()
constructor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]RTCErrorEvent()\f[B]\f[R] constructor creates a new
\f[CR]RTCErrorEvent\f[R] object.
.PP
Note that you will not normally create an object of this type yourself.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
new RTCErrorEvent(type, options)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]type\f[R]
A string with the name of the event.
This is usually \f[CR]\(dqerror\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]options\f[R]
An object that, \f[I]in addition to the properties defined in
\f[CI]Event()\f[I]\f[R], can have the following properties:
.RS
.TP
\f[B]error\f[R]
A \f[CR]RTCError\f[R] describing the cause and the location of the
error.
.RE
.SS Return value
A new \f[CR]RTCErrorEvent\f[R] object.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
This example shows how you might create an \f[CR]RTCErrorEvent\f[R]
instance.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// Construct an RTCError (used to initialize the event)\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] rtcError = \f[B]new\f[R] RTCError(
  {
    errorDetail: \(dqsdp\-syntax\-error\(dq,
    sdpLineNumber: 42,
  },
  \(dqSDP syntax error on line 42\(dq,
);

\f[I]// Construct the RTCErrorEvent, passing the RTCError\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] event = \f[B]new\f[R] RTCErrorEvent(\(dqerror\(dq, {
  error: rtcError, \f[I]// required\f[R]
  bubbles: \f[B]true\f[R], \f[I]// Optional (inherited from Event)\f[R]
  cancelable: \f[B]false\f[R], \f[I]// Optional (inherited from Event)\f[R]
});
.EE
